From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Ask again for a backhaul STA-MLD the controller never received

A node is asked for its backhaul STA-MLD only when the controller's own
bstamld config changed after that node last acked. A node that misses that
single window - it joined after the change, or its 0xE1 response was lost -
is never asked again. The bstamld table then holds no row for it for the
lifetime of the controller, while its links are demonstrably up.

Measured twice on bpi-4g2: on 2026-08-08, and again on 2026-08-09 after a cold
start in which that node happened to boot first and the controller last. Both
backhaul links associated on 5 and 6 GHz, the agent's own status carrying a
bsta_mld section, and nothing on record in the controller.

The fix asks again, bounded two ways. Only a node with a wireless uplink is
asked - for the root an empty row is the correct answer rather than a gap to
fill - and only once per holdoff, so a node that genuinely has nothing to say
costs one CMDU every two minutes instead of one on every tick.

The existing stale-config test moves into a named predicate beside the new
one, so the call site reads as the two reasons to ask rather than as one
timestamp comparison with a second case bolted on.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

--- a/src/cntlr.h
+++ b/src/cntlr.h
@@ -222,6 +222,11 @@
 	 * backhaul uplink -> single element (NULL until a 0xE1 TLV is parsed),
 	 * not a list. Mirror of apmld_list's lifecycle. */
 	struct wifi_bstamld_element *bstamld;
+
+	/* Controller uptime (s) before which this node is not asked for its
+	 * backhaul STA-MLD again. Zero means "at the next opportunity".
+	 * See cntlr_bstamld_requery_due() for why re-asking is needed. */
+	uint64_t bstamld_requery_at;
 #endif
 };
 
--- a/src/cntlr.c
+++ b/src/cntlr.c
@@ -1871,6 +1871,61 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+#if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
+/* A node is asked for its backhaul STA-MLD only when the controller's own
+ * bstamld config changed after that node last acked. A node that misses that
+ * single window - it joined after the change, or its 0xE1 response was lost -
+ * is never asked again, and the bstamld table holds no row for it while the
+ * links are demonstrably up.
+ *
+ * Measured twice on bpi-4g2 (2026-08-08, and again after the cold start of
+ * 2026-08-09): both backhaul links associated on 5 and 6 GHz, the agent's own
+ * status carrying a bsta_mld section, and nothing on record here.
+ */
+#define BSTAMLD_REQUERY_HOLDOFF 120	/* seconds between re-asks, per node */
+
+static bool node_has_wireless_uplink(const struct node *n)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
+		if (n->upstream_bssid[i])
+			return true;
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
+static bool cntlr_bstamld_config_stale(struct controller *c, struct node *n)
+{
+	return timestamp_less_than(&n->last_bstamld_ack, &c->cfg.last_bstamld_change) &&
+	       timestamp_greater_than(&n->last_cmdu, &c->cfg.last_bstamld_change);
+}
+
+/* Says yes at most once per holdoff per node, and reserves that slot as it
+ * answers: the caller sends on the strength of the answer, so the bookkeeping
+ * belongs with the decision rather than with the sending.
+ *
+ * A node with no wireless uplink is the root. For the root an empty row is the
+ * right answer rather than a gap to fill, so it is never asked.
+ */
+static bool cntlr_bstamld_requery_due(struct controller *c, struct node *n)
+{
+	if (n->bstamld)
+		return false;
+
+	if (!node_has_wireless_uplink(n))
+		return false;
+
+	if (c->uptime < n->bstamld_requery_at)
+		return false;
+
+	n->bstamld_requery_at = c->uptime + BSTAMLD_REQUERY_HOLDOFF;
+
+	return true;
+}
+#endif
+
 static void cntlr_renew_nodes_configuration(struct controller *c)
 {
 	trace("%s: --->\n", __func__);
@@ -1887,6 +1942,28 @@
 	/* When at least one running node was configured with older config, */
 	/* send autoconfig renew */
 	list_for_each_entry(node, &c->agentlist, list) {
+#if (EASYMESH_VERSION >= 6)
+		/* Deliberately not an arm of the chain below.
+		 *
+		 * The AP-MLD arm holds for a node until that node acks, and the
+		 * first arm leaves the loop outright, so a node can sit behind
+		 * either of them indefinitely. Asking from inside the chain
+		 * would reproduce the very gap this is here to close: a node
+		 * that is never reached is exactly the node whose row is
+		 * missing.
+		 *
+		 * info() rather than dbg() on purpose. This fires only while a
+		 * row is genuinely absent, which is either a node still coming
+		 * up or a bug worth seeing, and dbg is below the default level.
+		 */
+		if (cntlr_bstamld_requery_due(c, node)) {
+			info("%s: no backhaul STA-MLD on record for " MACFMT " - asking again\n",
+			     __func__, MAC2STR(node->almacaddr));
+
+			cntlr_send_bsta_mld_configuration_request(c, node);
+		}
+#endif
+
 		if (!timestamp_invalid(&node->last_config) &&
 		    timestamp_less_than(&node->last_config, &c->cfg.last_change) &&
 		    timestamp_greater_than(&node->last_cmdu, &c->cfg.last_change)) {
@@ -1916,8 +1993,7 @@
 			dbg("Found possibly unconfigured AP MLD node: "MACFMT"\n", MAC2STR(node->almacaddr));
 
 			cntlr_send_ap_mld_configuration_request(c, node);
-		} else if (timestamp_less_than(&node->last_bstamld_ack, &c->cfg.last_bstamld_change) &&
-			   timestamp_greater_than(&node->last_cmdu, &c->cfg.last_bstamld_change)) {
+		} else if (cntlr_bstamld_config_stale(c, node)) {
 
 			dbg("Found possibly unconfigured BSTA MLD node: "MACFMT"\n", MAC2STR(node->almacaddr));
 
